  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Given that $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],$ and ${A}^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q.$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's find the characteristic equation $\\text{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-8 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2}-5A-2 & =O.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by ${A}^{-1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1}({A}^{2}-5A-2) & ={A}^{-1}O \\\\ A-5I-2{A}^{-1} & =O \\\\ {A}^{-1} & =-\\frac{5}{2}I+\\frac{1}{2}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{5}{2},$ $q=\\frac{1}{2}$ and\n\n$$\np+q=-\\frac{5}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}=-2.\n$$",
